    {
      "type": "tweet",
      "id": "1984757066397814957",
      "url": "https://x.com/natalie_Zion_/status/1984757066397814957",
      "twitterUrl": "https://twitter.com/natalie_Zion_/status/1984757066397814957",
      "text": "Christian Persecution in the Middle East---\n\nChristian communities in the Middle East, once comprising a significant portion of the population, have faced severe persecution and demographic decline over the past century, exacerbated by ongoing conflicts, extremism, and discriminatory policies in several countries. \n\nAccording to recent reports, more than 380 million Christians worldwide experience high levels of persecution and discrimination for their faith, with the Middle East being a hotspot where violence, displacement, and systemic oppression have led to a sharp reduction in Christian populations. \n\nIn the early 20th century, Christians made up about 20% of the region's population; today, that figure has dropped to around 4%, with some areas approaching \"genocide\" levels, as seen in Iraq and Syria. \n\r\n\rThis decline is attributed to a mix of factors, including Islamist militancy, state-sponsored restrictions, civil wars, and foreign interventions that have destabilized the region. \n\nKey Countries and Trends in 2025; \n\nPersecution varies by country, but common issues include violence from extremist groups, legal discrimination, forced conversions, church demolitions, and social exclusion. Here's a breakdown based on 2025 data:\n\nEgypt: Home to the largest Christian population in the Middle East (primarily Coptic Orthodox), Egypt sees ongoing attacks on churches and individuals, often linked to sectarian tensions. Incidents include assaults on priests and demolitions of Christian sites, reflecting systemic discrimination under Islamic-majority governance. \n\r\n\rDespite constitutional protections, enforcement is weak, and Christians face intimidation in daily life.\n\nIraq and Syria: \n\nThese countries have experienced some of the most extreme persecution, with Christian populations plummeting due to ISIS attacks starting in the 2010s and the aftermath of the 2003 US invasion of Iraq, which removed Saddam Hussein's regime and led to chaos. \n\r\nIn Iraq, Christians dropped from 1.5 million in 2003 to fewer than 250,000 today, amid mass killings and displacements. \n\r\n Syria's civil war has similarly decimated communities, with reports of \"genocide\" levels of violence.\nIran: Strict enforcement of Islamic law leads to arrests, imprisonment, and executions for Christians, especially converts from Islam. \n\nHouse churches are raided, and evangelism is criminalized, contributing to high persecution scores on global indices.\n\nSaudi Arabia and Gulf States: \n\nIn Saudi Arabia, public Christian worship is banned, and expatriate Christians (mostly foreign workers) face severe restrictions. \n\nThe Arabian Peninsula's Christian presence is almost entirely migrant labourers from places like the Philippines and India, with little to no rights for local practice. \n\n However, the UAE stands out as more tolerant, not appearing on high-persecution lists. \n\nLebanon and Palestine: \n\nLebanon's Christian population has fallen from about 70% to 30% over the century, driven by wars, economic crises, and emigration. \n\r\nConversely, Israel is noted as the only Middle Eastern country where the Christian population is growing and protected, with freedom of worship. \n\nOther Regions: \n\nIn Yemen and Somalia (often grouped with broader Middle East/North Africa trends), al-Qaeda and al-Shabaab target Christians, making these among the most dangerous places globally.",
